"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,314\nEpisode 9\n\n3\n00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:09,236\nWhy we forget\n\n4\n00:00:12,036 --> 00:00:14,838\nEbbinghaus learned lists of nonsense syllables,\n\n5\n00:00:15,081 --> 00:00:17,744\nthen timed how long it took to relearn them.\n\n6\n00:00:18,177 --> 00:00:19,688\nMost of the loss came fast,\n\n7\n00:00:19,708 --> 00:00:21,032\nwithin the first day,\n\n8\n00:00:21,164 --> 00:00:22,203\nand then slowed.\n\n9\n00:00:22,788 --> 00:00:26,788\nA careful replication in 2015 found the same shape.\n\n10\n00:00:27,236 --> 00:00:29,734\nBut forgetting is not just time passing.\n\n11\n00:00:29,904 --> 00:00:31,658\nMuch of it is interference.\n\n12\n00:00:31,981 --> 00:00:33,996\nLooking across many lab studies,\n\n13\n00:00:34,184 --> 00:00:37,609\nUnderwood found that the more lists people had learned before,\n\n14\n00:00:37,823 --> 00:00:39,552\nthe more they forgot of the next one.\n\n15\n00:00:40,070 --> 00:00:41,808\nWith that interference removed,\n\n16\n00:00:42,036 --> 00:00:46,711\nforgetting over a day fell from about seventy five percent to about twenty five.\n\n17\n00:00:47,295 --> 00:00:51,717\nSimilar material competes, and new learning can crowd out the old.\n\n18\n00:00:51,887 --> 00:00:54,667\nAnd much of what seems forgotten is still there.\n\n19\n00:00:55,161 --> 00:01:00,555\nPeople who could not recall words freely recalled many more when given the category names.\n\n20\n00:01:00,922 --> 00:01:02,460\nThe memories were available,\n\n21\n00:01:02,462 --> 00:01:03,862\nbut not accessible.\n\n22\n00:01:04,032 --> 00:01:06,405\nMice show this at the level of cells.\n\n23\n00:01:06,726 --> 00:01:08,785\nAfter a drug blocked consolidation,\n\n24\n00:01:08,805 --> 00:01:11,339\nthe mice could no longer recall a fear.\n\n25\n00:01:11,676 --> 00:01:14,483\nBut when the engram cells were switched on with light,\n\n26\n00:01:14,566 --> 00:01:15,773\nthe memory came back.\n\n27\n00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:17,529\nThe trace was stored.\n\n28\n00:01:17,968 --> 00:01:19,664\nThe path to it was lost.\n\n29\n00:01:19,834 --> 00:01:21,779\nForgetting is not only failure.\n\n30\n00:01:22,246 --> 00:01:26,919\nLosing access to outdated details keeps current ones easy to find.\n\n31\n00:01:27,510 --> 00:01:32,490\nAnd a memory that has become harder to retrieve gains more from being retrieved again.\n\n32\n00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:33,464\nA caution.\n\n33\n00:01:33,852 --> 00:01:36,951\nMuch of this comes from word lists and animal studies.\n\n34\n00:01:37,346 --> 00:01:40,025\nHow fast we forget depends on the material,\n\n35\n00:01:40,231 --> 00:01:41,572\nhow well it was learned,\n\n36\n00:01:41,579 --> 00:01:42,958\nand how it is tested.\n\n37\n00:01:43,128 --> 00:01:43,908\nFor teaching.\n\n38\n00:01:44,332 --> 00:01:45,966\nExpect steep early loss,\n\n39\n00:01:45,986 --> 00:01:48,395\nand revisit material within days.\n\n40\n00:01:48,734 --> 00:01:52,283\nSeparate similar topics, or teach their differences directly.\n\n41\n00:01:52,656 --> 00:01:54,172\nAnd when students go blank,\n\n42\n00:01:54,247 --> 00:01:56,360\ngive a cue before giving the answer.\n\n43\n00:01:56,530 --> 00:01:58,465\nForgetting is rarely erasing.\n\n44\n00:01:58,812 --> 00:02:00,828\nMostly, the way back is lost.\n\n"
